<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Francisco Goya</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1746">1746</a>–<a href="/years/1828">1828</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Francisco José de Goya y Lucientes</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Francisco Goya was a Spanish romantic painter and printmaker, often called the last of the Old Masters and the first of the moderns. Born in Fuendetodos, Aragon, he became a court painter to the Spanish Crown in 1786 and in 1799 Primer Pintor de Cámara, the highest rank for a Spanish court painter. An illness in 1793 left him deaf, and his work grew darker, from the Caprichos and the Disasters of War prints to The Third of May 1808 and the Black Paintings of 1819–1823 on the walls of his house, the Quinta del Sordo. He left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in 1824 for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bordeaux">Bordeaux</a>, where he died in 1828.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/francisco-goya.jpg" alt="Francisco Goya" width="330" height="407" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Vicente López Portaña · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vicente_López_Portaña_-_el_pintor_Francisco_de_Goya.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Goya was born on 30 March 1746 in Fuendetodos, Aragon, to José Benito de Goya y Franque, a gilder who worked on the rebuilding of the Basilica of Our Lady of the Pillar in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaragoza">Zaragoza</a>, and Gracia de Lucientes y <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salvador">Salvador</a>. At school he formed a lifelong friendship with Martín Zapater, to whom he later wrote 131 letters.</p><p>At fourteen he studied under José Luzán and then went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a> to work with Anton Raphael Mengs, with whom he clashed. His entries for the Real Academia de Bellas Artes de San Fernando in 1763 and 1766 were rejected. He travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> at his own expense and married Josefa Bayeu, sister of the painter Francisco Bayeu, on 25 July 1773. Of their seven children only one, Javier, reached adulthood.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Bayeu's influence helped Goya win a commission for tapestry cartoons for the Royal Tapestry Factory. Over five years he designed some 42 patterns, mostly in a rococo style, for the walls of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-escorial">El Escorial</a> and the Palacio Real del Pardo. His portraits of the nobility and royal family are notable for their disinclination to flatter; Charles IV of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and His Family is now read by many as a satire on a corrupt court.</p><p>An undiagnosed illness between late 1792 and early 1793 left him deaf. While convalescing he painted eleven small pictures on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tin">tin</a>, including Yard with Lunatics, and in 1799 he published 80 aquatinted Caprichos, which include the caption &quot;The sleep of reason produces monsters&quot;.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>During the Peninsular War Goya stayed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a> and kept neutral; after the restoration of Ferdinand VII in 1814 he denied any involvement with the French. The Disasters of War etchings were not published until 1863, 35 years after his death.</p><p>In his seventies he withdrew to the Quinta del Sordo outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a> and painted fourteen Black Paintings in oil directly on its plaster walls. He did not intend them to be exhibited. About 1874 they were transferred to canvas, suffering heavy loss, and they now hang in the Museo del Prado. Goya left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bordeaux">Bordeaux</a> in October 1824, living there with Leocadia Weiss, his maid and relative. He died on 16 April 1828, and in 1919 his body was reburied in the Real Ermita de San Antonio de la <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a> in Madrid. Pablo Picasso and Salvador Dalí drew on the Caprichos and the Black Paintings.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Francisco Goya” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
